## The Problem: Object Reference Equality

React uses **shallow comparison** (`===`) to determine if props changed. When you pass an object as a prop, React compares **references**, not values.

```javascript
// This creates a NEW object on every render, even if values are identical
<MyComponent config={{ theme: 'dark', size: 'large' }} />
```

Every parent render creates a new object literal → new reference → React thinks props changed → child re-renders.

---

## Why This Happens

```javascript
function Parent() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  // ❌ New object reference created on every render
  const config = { theme: 'dark', size: 'large' };

  return (
    <>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>Count: {count}</button>
      <Child config={config} /> {/* Re-renders every time count changes */}
    </>
  );
}
```

---

## Solutions

### 1. `useMemo` — Memoize the object
```javascript
function Parent() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  // ✅ Same reference unless dependencies change
  const config = useMemo(() => ({
    theme: 'dark',
    size: 'large'
  }), []); // Empty array = created once

  return (
    <>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>Count: {count}</button>
      <Child config={config} />
    </>
  );
}
```

### 2. Move the object outside the component
```javascript
// ✅ Best option if the object doesn't depend on props/state
const CONFIG = { theme: 'dark', size: 'large' };

function Parent() {
  return <Child config={CONFIG} />;
}
```

### 3. `React.memo` on the child — Skip re-renders
```javascript
// ✅ Child only re-renders if props actually change
const Child = React.memo(function Child({ config }) {
  return <div>{config.theme}</div>;
});
```

> **Note:** `React.memo` still uses shallow comparison, so it won't help if you're passing a new object reference each time. Combine it with `useMemo`.

### 4. Pass primitive values instead of objects
```javascript
// ✅ Primitives compare by value, not reference
<Child theme="dark" size="large" />
```

---

## Complete Fix

```javascript
// Child component - memoized
const Child = React.memo(function Child({ config }) {
  console.log('Child rendered');
  return <div>{config.theme} - {config.size}</div>;
});

// Parent component
function Parent() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  // Stable reference with useMemo
  const config = useMemo(() => ({
    theme: 'dark',
    size: 'large'
  }), []);

  return (
    <>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>Count: {count}</button>
      <Child config={config} /> {/* ✅ No longer re-renders on count change */}
    </>
  );
}
```

---

## Decision Guide

| Situation | Solution |
|---|---|
| Object has no dependencies | Move outside component |
| Object depends on state/props | `useMemo` |
| Want to protect child component | `React.memo` |
| Can split the object into primitives | Pass individual props |

---

## Key Takeaway

> **Don't optimize prematurely.** Re-renders aren't always a problem — they're only expensive if the component does heavy work. Profile first with React DevTools before adding memoization, since `useMemo`/`React.memo` themselves have overhead.
